<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Hungarian Soviet Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1919 CE – 1919 CE · capital Budapest</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Communist regime of Béla Kun that ruled Hungary for 133 days in 1919, the second Soviet-style state in the world after Russia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vix-note">Vix Note</a> in March 1919 demanded further Hungarian withdrawals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihaly-karolyi">Mihály Károlyi's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hungarian-democratic-republic">Hungarian Democratic Republic</a> resigned. The Social Democrats merged with the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-kun">Béla Kun</a>, recently released from prison, and on 21 March 1919 they proclaimed a soviet republic, governed by a Revolutionary Governing Council nominally chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandor-garbai">Sándor Garbai</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The regime nationalised industry, banks, large estates and housing, but kept the estates as state farms rather than giving land to peasants, losing rural support. Revolutionary tribunals and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibor-szamuely">Tibor Szamuely's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenin-boys">Lenin Boys</a> carried out a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-terror-hungary">Red Terror</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-red-army">Hungarian Red Army</a> recaptured much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> in May and June, briefly creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/slovak-soviet-republic">Slovak Soviet Republic</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Withdrawal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> under Allied pressure and an offensive by the Romanian army brought collapse; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-kun">Kun</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> on 1 August 1919, and Romanian troops occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-terror-hungary">White Terror</a> followed, leading to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miklos-horthy">Miklós Horthy</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hungary-1867-1918">Kingdom of Hungary</a> (1920-1946).</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hungarian-soviet-republic.jpg" alt="Hungarian Soviet Republic" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hungary_(1919).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1919 CE – 1919 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Budapest</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Béla Kun, Sándor Garbai</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hungarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Hungary, Slovakia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Councils in Hungary</dd></dl></aside></div>
